@page "/settings/maps"
@attribute [Authorize]
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Components
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using BlazorDLR.Shared.State
@inject MapSourceState MapSources
@inject MapPackState Packs
@inject IDeviceSettings Settings
@inject ConfirmService Confirm
@inject TimeProvider Clock
@implements IAsyncDisposable

@*
	Which tiles go under every map in the app (§4.5, §18.6).

	A device preference, like the route style and the private area — two riders on the same
	ride can hold different answers and neither is wrong. It never reaches the server.

	The base map is only ever tiles, camera, rotation and attribution: every marker, rider pin
	and route is drawn by the Skia overlay on top (§4.5 v0.21). So nothing on this screen can
	change what a ride shows — only what it is drawn over.

	The preview is the point of the screen rather than decoration. A tile URL is the one
	setting in this app whose value is impossible to judge by reading it: the difference
	between a working template and a blank map is a character somewhere in the middle. The map
	below restyles the moment a source is applied, because RideMap listens to the same scoped
	state this page writes.
*@

<PageTitle>Maps — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

<div class="page page-medium form-page map-settings">
	<PageNav Title="Maps" BackHref="/settings" BackLabel="Back to settings" />

	<p class="lead">
		Where the map's tiles come from. Stored on this device and never sent to the server —
		your routes, markers and everyone's positions are drawn on top and do not change.
	</p>

	<fieldset>
		<legend>Tile source</legend>

		<label class="radio">
			<input type="radio" name="map-source" checked="@(_kind == MapSourceKind.Osm)"
				   @onchange="() => ChooseAsync(MapSourceKind.Osm)" />
			OpenStreetMap
		</label>
		<p class="hint indent">
			Free, no account, and what the app has always used. Needs a connection.
		</p>

		@*
			Offline is offered only where a pack can actually live — the phone (§18.6). The browser
			hosts used to show it disabled with a note saying why, on the reasoning that a rider who
			set it on their phone deserved an explanation here. That reasoning does not survive the
			screen it produced: this page is per device, the sentence explained an option that was
			never going to become available on this one, and the two hosts that show it are the two
			where the whole section below — packs, downloads, cartography — is inert.

			It stays enabled whenever it is shown, including on a phone holding no packs. Picking it
			stores nothing on its own (see ChooseAsync); what it does is reveal the download form,
			which is the only way a device with no packs ever gets one.
		*@
		@if (Packs.IsSupported)
		{
			<label class="radio">
				<input type="radio" name="map-source" checked="@(_kind == MapSourceKind.Offline)"
					   @onchange="() => ChooseAsync(MapSourceKind.Offline)" />
				Offline map pack
			</label>
			<p class="hint indent">@OfflineNote</p>
		}

		@* Which pack, when there is more than one. A radio inside a radio would be a mess, so the
		   packs are their own list and choosing one also selects the offline source — picking a
		   map is the whole intent, and making it two actions would be pedantry. *@
		@if (_kind == MapSourceKind.Offline && Packs.IsSupported && Packs.Packs.Count > 0)
		{
			<ul class="packs indent">
				@foreach (StoredMapPack pack in Packs.Packs)
				{
					<li class="@(_packId == pack.PackId ? "chosen" : null)">
						<label class="radio">
							<input type="radio" name="map-pack" checked="@(_packId == pack.PackId)"
								   @onchange="() => ChoosePackAsync(pack.PackId)" />
							<span class="name">@NameFor(pack.PackId)</span>
						</label>
						<span class="size">@MapPackDownloader.Describe(pack.SizeBytes)</span>
						<button type="button" class="remove" @onclick="() => DeletePackAsync(pack.PackId)"
								disabled="@Packs.IsDownloading"
								aria-label="@($"Delete {NameFor(pack.PackId)}")">Delete</button>
					</li>
				}
			</ul>
		}

		<label class="radio">
			<input type="radio" name="map-source" checked="@(_kind == MapSourceKind.Custom)"
				   @onchange="() => ChooseAsync(MapSourceKind.Custom)" />
			Another tile server
		</label>
		<p class="hint indent">
			Your own tile server, or a service you hold an account with. You are responsible for
			whatever that server's terms require.
		</p>
	</fieldset>

	@*
		Light or dark, for an offline pack only (§13 Q26).

		It is offered here rather than beside the other two sources because it is the only source
		that has the choice: a pack is vector, so the colour lives in a style document beside the
		archive and both ship with the app — switching costs no download and no second pack. OSM
		and a rider's own tile server hand back finished images, and there is nothing to restyle.

		Base map only. Routes, markers and rider pins are the Skia overlay on top (§4.5 v0.21) and
		do not follow this — a rider who wants a different line over a dark map has Route display.
	*@
	@if (_kind == MapSourceKind.Offline && Packs.IsSupported)
	{
		<fieldset class="appearance">
			<legend>Map colours</legend>

			<label class="radio">
				<input type="radio" name="map-theme" checked="@(_theme == MapTheme.Light)"
					   @onchange="() => ChooseThemeAsync(MapTheme.Light)" />
				Light
			</label>
			<p class="hint indent">
				Daylight cartography, and what a pack draws unless you change it. Easiest to read
				through a visor in the sun.
			</p>

			<label class="radio">
				<input type="radio" name="map-theme" checked="@(_theme == MapTheme.Dark)"
					   @onchange="() => ChooseThemeAsync(MapTheme.Dark)" />
				Dark
			</label>
			<p class="hint indent">
				The same map painted for night. Your routes and everyone's pins are drawn on top and
				keep their own colours — Route display is where those are set.
			</p>
		</fieldset>
	}

	@if (_kind == MapSourceKind.Custom)
	{
		@*
			Applied on a button rather than as you type. Restyling per keystroke would send a
			request for every half-finished URL to whatever host the prefix happened to resolve
			to, which is rude at best.
		*@
		<fieldset class="custom">
			<legend>Tile server</legend>

			<label>
				Tile URL
				<input @bind="_template" @bind:event="oninput" spellcheck="false"
					   autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off" @onblur="RememberAsync"
					   placeholder="https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.png" />
			</label>
			<p class="hint">
				Must be <code>https</code> and carry <code>{x}</code>, <code>{y}</code> and
				<code>{z}</code>. Plain <code>http</code> will not load — the app is served over a
				secure connection, so the phone blocks it.
			</p>

			<label>
				Attribution
				<input @bind="_attribution" @bind:event="oninput" spellcheck="false"
					   @onblur="RememberAsync" placeholder="© Example Maps" />
			</label>
			<p class="hint">
				Shown in the corner of every map. Most tile servers require a specific credit —
				check theirs.
			</p>

			<label>
				Deepest zoom
				<input type="number" @bind="_maxZoom" @bind:event="oninput" @onblur="RememberAsync"
					   min="@MapSource.MinAllowedZoom" max="@MapSource.MaxAllowedZoom" />
			</label>
			<p class="hint">
				How far in the server has tiles for. Set it too high and the map dissolves when you
				zoom in on a marker; too low and it stops sharpening early. 19 suits most.
			</p>

			@*
				The button is disabled until the two fields are right, and while it is, it says why.

				Shown from the moment the form opens rather than after a failed attempt. A disabled
				button with no explanation is the worst of the three states — the rider can see the
				control they want and not what is standing between them and it — and waiting for an
				attempt cannot help here, because there is no attempt to make: they cannot press it.

				`aria-describedby` rather than a live region: the text is present rather than
				announced, so a screen reader reads it when the button is reached, and it is not
				re-announced on every keystroke that leaves it unchanged.
			*@
			<button type="button" class="primary" disabled="@(!CustomIsUsable)"
					aria-describedby="@(CustomIsUsable ? null : "tile-server-problem")"
					@onclick="ApplyCustomAsync">
				Use this tile server
			</button>

			@if (!CustomIsUsable)
			{
				<p id="tile-server-problem" class="error-text">@CustomProblem</p>
			}
		</fieldset>
	}

	@*
		Adding a pack, from the catalogue the server publishes (§4.2). On the phone only — a browser
		has nowhere to put one (§18.6), and a list that downloads 300 MB into a tab that forgets it
		would be worse than absent.

		And only once offline is the chosen source, because that is the only state it is an answer
		to. A rider on OSM is not part-way through adding a pack; showing them a list of regions and
		a Download button makes the screen look like it has a job outstanding when it does not.

		This is the whole reason the offline radio stays enabled on a phone with no packs on it: the
		list is behind that radio, so a disabled radio would have sealed off the one route to getting
		a pack — the classic case of a feature you can only reach once you already have it.

		This replaced a link-and-a-name form. That form was how the offline path got tested before a
		catalogue existed, and it asked a rider to know a URL and to invent a name the store might
		then refuse — two ways to get a 300 MB download wrong before it started, both of them
		invisible until it finished. The catalogue answers both: the name and the id come from the
		publisher, and the only thing left to choose is which region.
	*@
	@if (Packs.IsSupported && _kind == MapSourceKind.Offline)
	{
		<fieldset class="catalogue">
			<legend>Add a map pack</legend>

			@*
				No refresh control. One was here, and a button offering to re-fetch a file that is
				rebuilt when somebody publishes an extract — weeks apart — invites the pulling that it
				can never reward. The catalogue is read the first time this form is opened and that
				copy is held until the app restarts, which is the whole of the policy.
			*@
			<p class="hint">
				Maps you can carry with you. Large — usually tens or hundreds of megabytes — so use
				Wi-Fi. A download that is interrupted carries on from where it stopped.
			</p>

			@*
				A banner rather than a branch, so the problem never takes a list away with it. The
				commonest cause by a distance is no signal, which is also exactly the person trying to
				download a map for later — and coming back to this form is what asks again, because a
				failed read is not a copy and is not held.
			*@
			@if (Packs.CatalogueProblem is { } catalogueProblem)
			{
				<p class="error-text">@catalogueProblem</p>
			}

			@if (Packs.IsReadingCatalogue)
			{
				<p class="status" role="status">Loading the maps on offer…</p>
			}
			else if (Packs.Offers.Count == 0 && Packs.CatalogueProblem is null)
			{
				<p class="hint">There are no maps on offer at the moment.</p>
			}
			else if (Packs.Offers.Count > 0)
			{
				@*
					Two dropdowns, not a row per region and not one dropdown of everything.

					The catalogue was eight states; it is now two hundred and thirty-odd areas across
					eighty-odd countries, and meant to keep growing. A list renders every one of them
					on a phone screen, which turns a settings page into a scroll and buries the
					source radios and the preview map under it. One select collapses that to a line,
					but a single line holding two hundred options is a native picker somebody spins
					through looking for their country — so the country is asked first and the area
					within it second, which is how the catalogue is organised anyway (§4.2).

					Country is not compulsory. "All countries" leaves the second dropdown holding the
					whole catalogue, which is what this screen did before and is still the shortest
					route for anybody who knows the area's name and not which country publishes it.

					Neither dropdown carries a search box, and one over each was here. They came out
					because what they filtered was already narrow: the area search only ever narrowed
					what the country above it had narrowed, and the country search filtered a list a
					rider scrolls straight to their own country in. Two text fields stacked above two
					pickers is a taller form on the screen least able to spare the height, and the
					country dropdown is the filter this list actually needed.
				*@
				<div class="picker">
					<div class="field">
						<span class="field-label" id="map-region-label">Country or region</span>

						<select class="regions" value="@_region" @onchange="ChooseRegion"
								aria-labelledby="map-region-label">
							<option value="">All countries</option>
							@foreach (string region in RegionsOnOffer)
							{
								<option value="@region">@DescribeRegion(region)</option>
							}
						</select>
					</div>

					<div class="field">
						<span class="field-label" id="map-area-label">Area</span>

						<select class="offers" @bind="_offerId" aria-labelledby="map-area-label">
							<option value="">Choose a map…</option>
							@foreach (MapPackOffer offer in InRegion)
							{
								<option value="@offer.Id">@Describe(offer)</option>
							}
						</select>
					</div>
				</div>

				@*
					One label, and nothing to press when the pack is already here.

					This offered "Download again" for a pack the phone was holding, which is a second
					several-hundred-megabyte transfer over the top of a file that is already good — and
					the rider who taps it is almost always the one who thinks the first one failed. The
					way to get a fresh copy is to delete the old one first, which the list above does,
					costs nothing, and makes the disc space it is about to spend visible before it is
					spent rather than after.
				*@
				<button type="button" class="download primary"
						disabled="@(Chosen is null || AlreadyHere || Packs.IsDownloading)"
						aria-describedby="@(AlreadyHere ? "pack-already-here" : null)"
						@onclick="DownloadChosenAsync">
					Download
				</button>

				@* Why the button is grey, said where a disabled control cannot say it itself. *@
				@if (Chosen is { } here && AlreadyHere)
				{
					<p id="pack-already-here" class="hint">@here.Name is already on this phone. Delete it from the list above to fetch it again.</p>
				}
			}

			@if (Packs.IsDownloading)
			{
				<div class="progress" role="status">
					@* The count sits inside the bar rather than after it — see the styles below. *@
					<div class="bar">
						<progress value="@(Packs.Progress?.Fraction ?? 0)" max="1"></progress>
						<span>@DownloadDescription</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			}

			@if (Packs.Status is { } packStatus)
			{
				<p class="status" role="status">@packStatus</p>
			}
		</fieldset>
	}

	@if (_status is not null)
	{
		<p class="status" role="status">@_status</p>
	}

	<section class="preview">
		<h2>Preview</h2>
		<p class="hint">
			The same base map every ride is drawn over. Pan and zoom it to check the tiles load at
			the detail you ride at — it opens where you left it last, or over your last ride.
		</p>
		<div class="preview-map">
			<RideMap Camera="_preview" AllowRotation="false" ShowPickingCursor="false"
					 OnViewportReported="OnPreviewMovedAsync" />
		</div>
	</section>
</div>

@code {
	/// <summary>
	/// Where the preview opens on a device that has neither moved it nor ever opened a live ride:
	/// the whole world.
	/// <para>
	/// The last of three answers, and the only one that is a constant. The preview's own stored
	/// camera comes first, then the ground the live ride map was last over
	/// (<see cref="BorrowLiveViewAsync"/>) — both of them places this rider knows, which is what the
	/// check they came to make needs.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// It is the world rather than a city when neither is on the device. A fixed city is an opinion
	/// this screen has no business holding: <c>GroupRideLive</c> falls back to one because it has a
	/// ride to show and must show it somewhere, while this map is a sample of a tile server and has
	/// no place in mind — and Sydney tells every rider who does not live there that the check starts
	/// with a pan. The world also answers the first question fastest, since at that zoom whether the
	/// tiles arrive at all is visible before touching anything.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly MapCamera WorldView = new(0, 0, 0);

	/// <summary>
	/// Where the preview is pointed.
	/// <para>
	/// Assigned once — from the device, after the first render — and never again. It is a parameter
	/// to <c>RideMap</c>, which pushes any change at the base map, so writing the rider's own pans
	/// back into it would fight the hand that made them: every drag would report a viewport, set the
	/// camera, and be re-applied a frame later. What the rider moves to is held in
	/// <see cref="_previewNow"/> instead, which nothing renders.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private MapCamera _preview = WorldView;

	/// <summary>
	/// The latest view the preview has reported, saved or not. Null until it reports one.
	/// </summary>
	private MapCamera? _previewNow;

	/// <summary>What <see cref="_previewNow"/> was when the device was last written.</summary>
	private MapCamera? _previewSaved;

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the stored camera has been read yet. Until it has, the preview's own reports are
	/// ignored.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Not a detail.</strong> A child renders before its parent, so <c>RideMap</c> attaches
	/// and announces the view it opened on — the world — while this page is still one render away
	/// from reading the device. Without this flag that announcement is a pan as far as the handler
	/// below can tell, and it writes the world over the ground the rider left, every visit. The
	/// feature would have failed in exactly the case it exists for and looked like a store that does
	/// not persist.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool _previewRestored;

	/// <summary>
	/// The clock reading at the last write, for the throttle below. Never compared against anything
	/// but itself, so a device whose clock jumps costs at most one extra write.
	/// </summary>
	private DateTimeOffset _lastPreviewSaveUtc;

	/// <summary>
	/// How often panning the preview is allowed to reach the device store.
	/// <para>
	/// The map reports a viewport on every frame of a drag, and on the web every write is a JS
	/// interop round trip. Nothing reads this value until the next visit, so writing it at frame rate
	/// buys nothing — and what the throttle holds back is not dropped, because
	/// <see cref="DisposeAsync"/> writes whatever is pending on the way out.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly TimeSpan PreviewSaveInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);

	private MapSourceKind _kind = MapSourceKind.Osm;
	private string _template = string.Empty;
	private string _attribution = string.Empty;
	private int _maxZoom = MapSource.OsmMaxZoom;

	/// <summary>Which downloaded pack is selected, when the offline source is.</summary>
	private string? _packId;

	/// <summary>
	/// Which cartography an offline pack draws with. Held on the page rather than read from the
	/// source each time, so a rider can pick dark on a phone whose pack is still downloading and
	/// have it apply to the pack they choose afterwards.
	/// </summary>
	private MapTheme _theme = MapTheme.Light;

	private string? _status;

	/// <summary>
	/// Which country the rider has narrowed to, or empty for all of them.
	/// <para>
	/// Empty is a real answer rather than a prompt: it leaves the second dropdown holding the whole
	/// catalogue, which is what this screen offered before there were countries in it and is still the
	/// shortest route for somebody who knows the area's name — the catalogue files Bangladesh under
	/// India and Sardinia under Italy, and neither is wrong or guessable.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string _region = string.Empty;

	/// <summary>Which pack is picked in the dropdown, by catalogue id, or empty for none.</summary>
	private string _offerId = string.Empty;

	/// <summary>
	/// The countries the catalogue offers anything in, in the order the first dropdown lists them.
	/// <para>
	/// Distinct rather than counted from a table of countries: this screen knows about the countries
	/// its offers name and no others, so a catalogue that publishes one region or fifty needs no
	/// change here. The order comes from <c>MapPackCatalogue</c>, which sorts by country and then by
	/// name — with the leftovers pushed to the end, because a rider scrolling for their country is
	/// not scrolling for the bucket of packs that named none.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<string> RegionsOnOffer =>
		[.. Packs.Offers
			.Select(offer => offer.Region)
			.Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
			.OrderBy(region => region == MapPackCatalogue.UnknownRegion)];

	/// <summary>The packs in the chosen country, which is what the second dropdown lists.</summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<MapPackOffer> InRegion =>
		_region.Length == 0
			? Packs.Offers
			: [.. Packs.Offers.Where(offer =>
				string.Equals(offer.Region, _region, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))];

	/// <summary>
	/// One country as a line in the first dropdown: its name and how many areas are under it.
	/// <para>
	/// The count is there because it is the thing that decides whether opening the second dropdown is
	/// worth doing — Australia is eight states and France is eight regions, but Greenland is one map,
	/// and knowing that before the tap saves the tap.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="region">The country.</param>
	private string DescribeRegion(string region)
	{
		int count = Packs.Offers.Count(offer =>
			string.Equals(offer.Region, region, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

		return count == 1 ? $"{region} — 1 map" : $"{region} — {count} maps";
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Narrowing to a country, which also clears what was picked under the last one.
	/// <para>
	/// Cleared rather than kept and filtered out. A selection the rider can no longer see is the
	/// state a disabled Download button cannot explain.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="changed">What the dropdown now holds.</param>
	private void ChooseRegion(ChangeEventArgs changed)
	{
		_region = changed.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;
		_offerId = string.Empty;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The pack the Download button would fetch, or <c>null</c> when none is picked.
	/// <para>
	/// Resolved against <see cref="InRegion"/> rather than the whole catalogue, because a selection
	/// outside the chosen country is not one the rider can see — and a button that downloads
	/// something invisible is worse than a disabled one.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private MapPackOffer? Chosen =>
		_offerId.Length == 0 ? null : InRegion.FirstOrDefault(offer => offer.Id == _offerId);

	/// <summary>
	/// Whether the picked pack is one this device already holds, which is what takes the Download
	/// button out of use.
	/// <para>
	/// A second copy over the top of a good one is hundreds of megabytes for no map the rider does
	/// not already have. Deleting is the way to a fresh one, and it is a tap away in the list above —
	/// which also means the space is freed before the download rather than doubled during it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private bool AlreadyHere => Chosen is { } offer && Packs.Stored(offer.Id) is not null;

	/// <summary>
	/// One pack as a line in the dropdown: what it is, what it costs, and whether it is already here.
	/// <para>
	/// All three on the option itself because an option is all a native picker shows — there is no
	/// room beside it for the size column a list had, and the size is the number somebody decides on.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="offer">The pack on offer.</param>
	private string Describe(MapPackOffer offer) =>
		Packs.Stored(offer.Id) is not null
			? $"{offer.Name} — {MapPackDownloader.Describe(offer.SizeBytes)} (on this phone)"
			: $"{offer.Name} — {MapPackDownloader.Describe(offer.SizeBytes)}";

	/// <summary>
	/// The small print under the offline radio. Only rendered on a host that can hold a pack, so
	/// the two cases left are "none yet" and "here is what it does".
	/// </summary>
	private string OfflineNote => Packs.Packs.Count == 0
		? "No map packs on this phone yet. Pick this to add one."
		: "Draws from a pack on this phone. Works with no signal at all.";

	/// <summary>
	/// What to call a pack that is already on the device: the catalogue's name for it, falling back
	/// to the id.
	/// <para>
	/// The fallback is the ordinary case rather than the odd one — the catalogue is only read once
	/// the rider picks the offline radio, and a phone with no signal is exactly the phone this
	/// feature exists for. <c>au-nsw</c> beside a size is still a legible answer to "what is on my
	/// phone and can I delete it", which is what that list is for.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="packId">The stored pack's id.</param>
	private string NameFor(string packId) =>
		Packs.Offers.FirstOrDefault(offer => offer.Id == packId)?.Name ?? packId;

	/// <summary>
	/// How far the download has got, as a rider reads it. Falls back to a bare byte count when the
	/// host did not send a length — some do not, and a progress bar that cannot fill is better
	/// than a number that never appears.
	/// </summary>
	private string DownloadDescription => Packs.Progress is not { } progress
		? "Starting…"
		: progress.TotalBytes is { } total
			? $"{MapPackDownloader.Describe(progress.BytesReceived)} of {MapPackDownloader.Describe(total)}"
			: MapPackDownloader.Describe(progress.BytesReceived);

	private bool CustomIsUsable =>
		MapSource.IsUsableTemplate(_template) && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_attribution);

	/// <summary>
	/// Why the tile server cannot be applied yet, in the rider's terms.
	/// <para>
	/// One sentence naming <em>this</em> URL's defect rather than restating the rule. The hint
	/// under the field already says what a good template looks like, and a rider staring at a
	/// disabled button has read it — what they cannot see is which part of what they typed is the
	/// problem, and on a URL long enough to need a tile server it is not obvious by looking.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private string CustomProblem
	{
		get
		{
			string template = _template.Trim();

			if (template.Length == 0)
			{
				return "Enter the tile server's URL above.";
			}

			if (!template.StartsWith("https://", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
			{
				// The commonest paste by a distance, and the one whose failure is invisible: plain
				// http is mixed content, so the tiles are blocked by the WebView rather than by us.
				return "The tile URL has to start with https:// — a plain http server is blocked before it is asked for a tile.";
			}

			// Named individually because a template is normally missing exactly one, and "contains
			// {x}, {y} and {z}" sends the rider back to compare three things against a long string.
			string[] missing = new[] { "{x}", "{y}", "{z}" }
				.Where(placeholder => !template.Contains(placeholder, StringComparison.Ordinal))
				.ToArray();

			if (missing.Length > 0)
			{
				return $"The tile URL is missing {string.Join(" and ", missing)}.";
			}

			return "Add the attribution the tile server asks to be credited with.";
		}
	}

	protected override void OnInitialized()
	{
		MapSources.Changed += OnSourceChanged;

		// The download runs in a scoped state rather than in this page, so it survives the rider
		// navigating away mid-transfer. That is also why the progress has to arrive by event.
		Packs.Changed += OnPacksChanged;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Reads the device after first render — on the web the store is behind JS interop, which does
	/// not exist during the prerender pass. Idempotent, so the preview map below reading it too
	/// costs nothing.
	/// </summary>
	protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
	{
		if (!firstRender)
		{
			return;
		}

		await MapSources.LoadAsync();
		await Packs.LoadAsync();

		// What was last typed into either half of this screen, so a rider comes back to their own
		// tile server and their own pack link rather than to two empty boxes and a long URL to
		// remember. Read before Adopt, which then overwrites the tile fields from the source that
		// is actually applied — those agree, because applying one writes this too.
		Remember(RememberedMapSetup.Decode(await Settings.GetAsync(RememberedMapSetup.StorageKey)));

		// Nothing panned here yet: borrow the ground the rider was last riding over instead of
		// opening on the world. See BorrowLiveViewAsync.
		if (_previewNow is null)
		{
			await BorrowLiveViewAsync();
		}

		// Only now may the preview's reports count as the rider moving it. Set whether or not there
		// was a camera to restore: a device that stored none still wants the first pan kept.
		_previewRestored = true;

		// Adopted here rather than left to the Changed handler. LoadAsync only raises when it was
		// the read that resolved the value — a rider arriving from another screen that already
		// read the device gets no event at all, and this form would open on the default while the
		// map below it drew something else.
		Adopt(MapSources.Effective);
		EnsureCatalogue();
		StateHasChanged();
	}

	private void OnSourceChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(() =>
	{
		Adopt(MapSources.Effective);
		EnsureCatalogue();
		StateHasChanged();
	});

	/// <summary>
	/// Asks for the list of packs on offer, when this screen is in a state that shows it.
	/// <para>
	/// Called from every path that can put the form on the offline source, not only from the radio.
	/// A rider who already drew with a pack last time arrives with offline <em>adopted</em> rather
	/// than chosen — no tap, no <c>ChooseAsync</c> — and the first version of this hung the fetch off
	/// the tap alone, so the one person most likely to want a second region was the one who had to
	/// select OpenStreetMap and come back to see the list at all.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Not awaited: the list is about what could be downloaded, and nothing already on screen depends
	/// on it. It re-renders when it lands, through <c>Packs.Changed</c>. A successful read is held for
	/// the rest of the launch, so calling this on every render costs one request in total.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>A read that failed is not retried from here</strong>, which is what
	/// <paramref name="deliberate"/> separates. This runs again on every theme change and every pack
	/// selection — those are re-renders of a form the rider is already looking at, and asking a host
	/// that just refused at each of them is a retry loop on the connection least able to serve one.
	/// Choosing the offline radio is a different thing: it is somebody arriving at this form on
	/// purpose, which with no refresh control is the one gesture that means "try again".
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="deliberate">
	/// True when the rider just picked the offline source themselves, which asks again after a failure.
	/// </param>
	private void EnsureCatalogue(bool deliberate = false)
	{
		if (_kind != MapSourceKind.Offline || !Packs.IsSupported)
		{
			return;
		}

		if (!deliberate && Packs.CatalogueProblem is not null)
		{
			return;
		}

		_ = Packs.LoadCatalogueAsync();
	}

	private void OnPacksChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

	/// <summary>Fills the form from what this device remembers being typed into it.</summary>
	/// <param name="remembered">The stored draft, or null on a device that has typed nothing.</param>
	private void Remember(RememberedMapSetup? remembered)
	{
		if (remembered is not { } draft)
		{
			return;
		}

		_template = draft.TileTemplate ?? string.Empty;
		_attribution = draft.TileAttribution ?? string.Empty;
		_maxZoom = draft.TileMaxZoom;

		if (draft.PreviewCamera is not { } camera)
		{
			return;
		}

		// One of only two assignments to _preview — see the field, and BorrowLiveViewAsync below.
		// RideMap has already opened on the world by now, and it handles a camera that arrives after
		// its first render: the parameter change is pushed at the base map, which is the one move
		// this restore needs.
		_preview = camera;
		_previewNow = camera;
		_previewSaved = camera;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Points the preview at wherever the live ride map was last looking, for a device that has never
	/// moved the preview itself.
	/// <para>
	/// The world is a poor first view for the job this map has. A rider is here to judge whether a
	/// tile source draws the ground they ride over — at the detail they ride at — and at zoom 0
	/// nothing on it is ground they can recognise, so the check starts with a pan and a pinch every
	/// time. The one place on the device that already knows which ground that is is
	/// <see cref="LiveMapView"/>, written by the live map they were just on.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>The ride the view belongs to is not checked</strong>, which is the opposite of what
	/// the live page does with the same value. There, applying one ride's camera to another opens
	/// Melbourne over Sydney and misrepresents the ride on screen. Here there is no ride to
	/// misrepresent — this map is a tile sample, and any ride the rider has opened is ground they
	/// know. Refusing a stale <c>RideId</c> would send them back to the world for no gain.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Nothing is written as a result. <see cref="_previewSaved"/> is set to the borrowed camera so
	/// that leaving without touching the map stores nothing — the borrow stays a borrow, and the next
	/// visit takes whatever the live map says <em>then</em> rather than freezing today's answer into
	/// the preview's own slot. The first pan is what makes it this screen's, through the ordinary
	/// path.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task BorrowLiveViewAsync()
	{
		LiveMapView? live = LiveMapView.Decode(await Settings.GetAsync(LiveMapView.StorageKey));

		// Re-normalised rather than trusted: LiveMapView clamps to what a live map offers, and this
		// is the range the preview's own slot is kept in. They agree today; a camera that fails is
		// simply not borrowed, and the world is already on screen.
		if (RememberedMapSetup.NormalisedCamera(live?.ToCamera()) is not { } camera)
		{
			return;
		}

		_preview = camera;
		_previewNow = camera;
		_previewSaved = camera;
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The preview reported a new view — a pan, a pinch, or the base map settling after it opened.
	/// Held, and let through to the device at most every <see cref="PreviewSaveInterval"/>.
	/// </summary>
	/// <param name="viewport">Where the base map is now looking.</param>
	private Task OnPreviewMovedAsync(MapViewport viewport)
	{
		// A viewport with no canvas is a map that has not laid out yet, and its centre is not a place.
		// And nothing is written before the stored camera has been read — see _previewRestored.
		if (!_previewRestored || viewport is not { CanvasWidthPx: > 0 })
		{
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		MapCamera? moved = RememberedMapSetup.NormalisedCamera(
			new MapCamera(viewport.CentreLatitude, viewport.CentreLongitude, viewport.ZoomLevel));

		if (moved is null || moved == _previewSaved)
		{
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		_previewNow = moved;

		DateTimeOffset stamp = Clock.GetUtcNow();

		if (stamp - _lastPreviewSaveUtc < PreviewSaveInterval)
		{
			return Task.CompletedTask;
		}

		_lastPreviewSaveUtc = stamp;
		return RememberAsync();
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Stores what is in the boxes right now.
	/// <para>
	/// Called when a field loses focus and before the tile server is applied, rather than on every
	/// keystroke: a device write per character is a JS interop round trip per character on the web,
	/// for a value nobody reads until the next visit. Applying saves first because a tap on a button
	/// does not always blur the field it was typed into.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// Whatever is there is kept, finished or not — see <see cref="RememberedMapSetup"/>. An empty
	/// form removes the key rather than storing three blanks.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The preview camera rides along with it. One key holds the whole screen's memory, so a rider
	/// who pans the map and then types a URL writes both at once rather than racing two keys — and a
	/// blur that saves a tile server also banks whatever panning the throttle was still holding.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task RememberAsync()
	{
		RememberedMapSetup draft = new(
			_template.Trim(),
			_attribution.Trim(),
			_maxZoom,
			_previewNow);

		_previewSaved = draft.PreviewCamera;

		if (draft.IsEmpty)
		{
			await Settings.RemoveAsync(RememberedMapSetup.StorageKey);
			return;
		}

		await Settings.SetAsync(RememberedMapSetup.StorageKey, draft.Encode());
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Brings the form into line with what is stored. The custom fields are only overwritten from
	/// a custom source: a rider who has typed a URL, switched to OSM to compare, and switched back
	/// should find their text still there.
	/// <para>
	/// Callers pass <c>Effective</c> rather than <c>Chosen</c>, which matters on the browser hosts
	/// alone: they no longer render an offline radio at all, so adopting a stored offline source
	/// there would leave the group with nothing checked. Effective is what those hosts are actually
	/// drawing with, and on a phone the two agree — a deleted pack still reads as offline, which is
	/// what keeps the pack list on screen.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private void Adopt(MapSource source)
	{
		_kind = source.Kind;

		if (source.Kind == MapSourceKind.Custom)
		{
			_template = source.UrlTemplate ?? string.Empty;
			_attribution = source.Attribution ?? string.Empty;
			_maxZoom = source.MaxZoom;
		}

		if (source.Kind == MapSourceKind.Offline)
		{
			_packId = source.PackId;
			_theme = source.Theme;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Picking a radio. OSM applies at once — there is nothing to fill in — while Custom only
	/// moves the form, because a source with no URL in it yet cannot draw anything.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ChooseAsync(MapSourceKind kind)
	{
		_kind = kind;
		_status = null;

		if (kind == MapSourceKind.Osm)
		{
			await MapSources.SetAsync(MapSource.Default);
			_status = "Using OpenStreetMap.";
			return;
		}

		if (kind == MapSourceKind.Offline)
		{
			// The list of packs on offer is fetched here rather than when the page opens: it is a
			// request to a host that is not the API, and only somebody adding a pack is going to look
			// at it. See EnsureCatalogue — this is one of three paths into the offline form, and the
			// only one that is a tap, which is why it is the one allowed to ask again after a failure.
			EnsureCatalogue(deliberate: true);

			// One pack on the device is not a choice worth making the rider make. Several, and the
			// list below is where they pick; until they do, nothing is stored — an offline source
			// with no pack id cannot draw a map and MapSource refuses it.
			if (Packs.Packs.Count == 1)
			{
				await ChoosePackAsync(Packs.Packs[0].PackId);
			}
		}
	}

	/// <summary>Selects a downloaded pack, which is also what turns the offline source on.</summary>
	private async Task ChoosePackAsync(string packId)
	{
		_kind = MapSourceKind.Offline;
		_packId = packId;

		await MapSources.SetAsync(MapSource.OfflinePack(packId, _theme));
		_status = $"Using {packId}. This map works with no signal.";
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Light or dark for the offline map (§13 Q26).
	/// <para>
	/// Applied at once, unlike the tile-server form: there is nothing to fill in and nothing to get
	/// wrong, and the preview below is the whole reason a rider is looking at this. It restyles the
	/// moment the source changes, because <c>RideMap</c> listens to the same scoped state.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// With no pack selected yet the choice is only remembered, not stored. An offline source needs
	/// an archive behind it — <c>MapSource.Normalised</c> refuses one without — so the theme is
	/// carried into whichever pack is picked next rather than being lost for having been chosen
	/// first.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task ChooseThemeAsync(MapTheme theme)
	{
		_theme = theme;

		if (_packId is not { } packId)
		{
			return;
		}

		await MapSources.SetAsync(MapSource.OfflinePack(packId, theme));
		_status = theme == MapTheme.Dark
			? "Dark map. Your routes and pins keep their own colours."
			: "Light map.";
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Fetches one of the packs on offer, filing it under the catalogue's id.
	/// <para>
	/// Nothing is selected as a result. A rider adding Victoria while riding on the New South Wales
	/// pack should keep drawing the map they are looking at — the new one appears in the list above
	/// and switching to it is a separate, deliberate tap.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	/// <summary>
	/// Fetches whatever the dropdown is showing. The button is disabled with nothing picked, so this
	/// is a guard against a programmatic caller rather than a path a rider can take.
	/// </summary>
	private async Task DownloadChosenAsync()
	{
		if (Chosen is { } offer)
		{
			await DownloadAsync(offer);
		}
	}

	/// <param name="offer">Which pack.</param>
	private async Task DownloadAsync(MapPackOffer offer)
	{
		// Nothing is checked about the URL here on purpose. This guarded on a "can it be fetched"
		// flag of its own once, which meant a row the screen was willing to draw a button for could
		// still be one this method refused — silently, because a return is not a message. The
		// downloader owns that rule (MapPackDownloader.IsFetchable), refuses before opening a
		// connection, and says why through Packs.Status, which is already on screen below.
		//
		// The button is disabled while another transfer runs, so this is a guard against a
		// programmatic caller rather than a path a rider can take; MapPackState refuses a second
		// download anyway.
		if (Packs.IsDownloading)
			return;

		await Packs.DownloadAsync(offer);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Removes a pack, and stops drawing with it if it was the one selected — the alternative is a
	/// stored source pointing at a file that is gone, which falls back silently and looks like the
	/// delete did nothing.
	/// <para>
	/// <strong>Asked first.</strong> Delete sits in a row of small controls next to a radio somebody
	/// taps to choose a map, on a phone, often in gloves — and what it throws away is the single
	/// largest thing this app puts on the device and the one thing a rider went to some trouble to
	/// get. The cost of a mis-tap is not the file, it is the several hundred megabytes and the Wi-Fi
	/// they have to find again, which on a trip is not always available at all.
	/// </para>
	/// <para>
	/// The size is in the question because it is the number that makes the consequence concrete, and
	/// because it is what the rider came to this screen to reclaim if the phone said it was full.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task DeletePackAsync(string packId)
	{
		StoredMapPack? pack = Packs.Stored(packId);

		if (!await Confirm.AskAsync(
			$"Delete {NameFor(packId)}?",
			pack is null
				? "The map is removed from this phone. Downloading it again needs a connection."
				: $"{MapPackDownloader.Describe(pack.SizeBytes)} is freed on this phone. Downloading "
					+ "it again needs a connection, and on Wi-Fi it is not quick.",
			confirmText: "Delete",
			danger: true))
		{
			return;
		}

		await Packs.DeleteAsync(packId);

		if (_packId == packId)
		{
			_packId = null;
			_kind = MapSourceKind.Osm;
			await MapSources.ResetAsync();
		}
	}

	private async Task ApplyCustomAsync()
	{
		// The button is disabled while this is false, so this is a guard against a programmatic
		// caller rather than a path the rider can take — the reason is already on screen.
		if (!CustomIsUsable)
		{
			return;
		}

		await RememberAsync();

		try
		{
			await MapSources.SetAsync(MapSource.Custom(_template.Trim(), _attribution.Trim(), _maxZoom));
			_status = "Tile server applied. The preview below is using it.";
		}
		catch (ArgumentException exception)
		{
			// MapSource.Normalised refused it after the form thought it was fine. Say what the
			// state said rather than inventing a friendlier sentence that discards it.
			_status = exception.Message;
		}
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// Unhooks, and writes whatever the throttle was still holding.
	/// <para>
	/// This is what makes <see cref="PreviewSaveInterval"/> a throttle rather than a way of losing
	/// the last pan: leaving the screen is the commonest thing a rider does immediately after moving
	/// the preview, and it is exactly the moment the held value would otherwise go.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		MapSources.Changed -= OnSourceChanged;
		Packs.Changed -= OnPacksChanged;

		if (_previewNow != _previewSaved)
		{
			await RememberAsync();
		}
	}
}

<style>
	/* The hint belonging to a radio, indented under it so it reads as that option's small
	   print rather than as the next thing on the form. */
	.map-settings .hint.indent {
		margin: 0.1rem 0 0.9rem 1.75rem;
	}

	.map-settings fieldset.custom,
	.map-settings fieldset.appearance,
	.map-settings fieldset.catalogue {
		margin-top: 1rem;
	}

	/* The packs on offer: country, then the area within it, each a search box and a dropdown and
	   each full width. Two lines whatever the catalogue holds — which is the point, since this
	   list is meant to grow past what a phone screen can show without burying the source radios
	   and the preview below. */
	.map-settings .picker {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.9rem;
		margin: 0.75rem 0 0.75rem;
	}

	/* One question and the controls that answer it, held together so the gap between country and
	   area is wider than the gap between a search box and the dropdown it filters. */
	.map-settings .picker .field {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.5rem;
	}

	.map-settings .picker .field-label {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

	.map-settings .picker .field .hint {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.map-settings .picker .regions,
	.map-settings .picker .offers {
		width: 100%;
		/* The same 44 px target as everything else pressed in a hurry. A select is a tap that opens
		   the platform's own picker, and a small one on a phone in gloves is a mis-tap. */
		min-height: 44px;
		padding: 0 0.6rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border-strong);
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: var(--dlr-bg);
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		font: inherit;
	}

	/* The one control on this screen that commits a rider to hundreds of megabytes, so it carries
	   the accent that .primary in app.css gives it rather than the outline the secondary buttons
	   here have. Only the tap target is set locally — the colours, and the grey it turns when
	   nothing is picked, come from app.css so this and "Use this tile server" cannot disagree. */
	.map-settings .download.primary {
		min-height: 44px;
	}

	/* The packs on this device. A row is a choice, a size and a way out of it — nothing else,
	   because this is the screen a rider comes to when the phone says it is full. */
	.map-settings .packs {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0 0 0.9rem 1.75rem;
		padding: 0;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.35rem;
	}

	.map-settings .packs li {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		padding: 0.4rem 0.6rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		border-radius: 6px;
	}

	.map-settings .packs li.chosen {
		border-color: var(--dlr-accent);
		background: var(--dlr-accent-soft);
	}

	.map-settings .packs label.radio {
		flex: 1;
		margin: 0;
		min-width: 0;
	}

	.map-settings .packs .name {
		overflow: hidden;
		text-overflow: ellipsis;
	}

	.map-settings .packs .size {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.map-settings .packs .remove {
		flex-shrink: 0;
		padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
		background: transparent;
		border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border-strong);
		border-radius: 4px;
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		font: inherit;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.map-settings .packs .remove:disabled {
		opacity: 0.5;
		cursor: default;
	}

	.map-settings .progress {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
	}

	/* The bar and the count it belongs to, stacked rather than sat side by side. This is the one
	   thing in the app a rider watches for minutes at a stretch — often on a phone propped up
	   across the room while the Wi-Fi does its work — so the bar is tall enough to read from
	   there, and the number is on it rather than beside it: a count in the middle of the thing
	   that is moving is read without moving the eye, and it leaves the bar the full width. */
	.map-settings .progress .bar {
		position: relative;
		flex: 1;
		min-width: 0;
		display: flex;
	}

	.map-settings .progress progress {
		flex: 1;
		min-width: 0;
		/* Twice the ~1rem a native bar draws itself at, which is also what makes room for the
		   text over it. The appearance reset is what lets the height take effect at all —
		   WebKit keeps drawing its own inset groove at its own size otherwise — and once it is
		   reset the fill and the track have to be painted here too. */
		height: 3rem;
		appearance: none;
		-webkit-appearance: none;
		border: none;
		border-radius: 6px;
		overflow: hidden;
		background: var(--dlr-surface-2);
		color: var(--dlr-accent); /* IE/legacy fill, harmless elsewhere */
	}

	.map-settings .progress progress::-webkit-progress-bar {
		background: var(--dlr-surface-2);
		border-radius: 6px;
	}

	.map-settings .progress progress::-webkit-progress-value {
		background: var(--dlr-accent);
	}

	.map-settings .progress progress::-moz-progress-bar {
		background: var(--dlr-accent);
	}

	/* Centred over the whole bar, so it does not move as the fill passes under it. Off-white
	   rather than the muted grey it was: it now has to read against both the accent fill and the
	   track behind it. Pointer events off so it can never swallow a tap. */
	.map-settings .progress .bar span {
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		pointer-events: none;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.map-settings .hint code {
		font-size: 0.85em;
